WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
SmallDocs is a tool which lets any coding agent
create a document and open it in the browser.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.760
So, for example, here I've been working on an
application,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.480
I'm thinking of installing OpenID Connect for
users,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.420
and I've got my agent to write a document for
me.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.140
And it opens in the browser.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.140
Or I'm on a coding agent, for example, one of
the more UI ones,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.040
and I'm working with an agent on something,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.100
and I want to read about it in a cleaner
environment,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.120
and the agent can open a document here.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.600
You can say, "Open a doc to explain this,"

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.450
or "Open a small doc or sdoc to explain that."

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.600
And it's good, if you get used to the tool, to
be specific.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.000
So you could say, "I want a Mermaid diagram for
these three things,"

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.700
or "I want a slide to show me this idea broken
down into single parts,"

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.900
or "I want a spreadsheet that does this," or
whatever.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.367
So the more information you give it, the more
the document will look like what you're
imagining.

00:01:00.387 --> 00:01:04.267
So, underneath these documents they are...

00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:10.207
They're markdown files, so they can be saved in
your repository or wherever they are,

00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:13.267
but they're also saved in our cloud as well,
which I'll get on to in a moment.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:19.527
The thinking behind SmallDocs is that these
chat areas where we do all of our work

00:01:19.527 --> 00:01:23.507
today, they become very busy, very filled, and
very cluttered.

00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:26.227
They're rushing along, and you come up with
another question you want to ask,

00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:28.347
and you ask your agent, and the chat moves on.

00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:32.727
But sometimes your understanding isn't moving
along at the same pace as the chat.

00:01:32.787 --> 00:01:35.627
So there's a separation for me of the work
which is happening in these chats,

00:01:35.647 --> 00:01:37.767
and the understanding which I do in SmallDocs.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:41.827
And SmallDocs are designed to be a calm, clean,
fast,

00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:45.567
lightweight, and spacious place to read and
gather thoughts,

00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:49.647
but as well as be extremely flexible documents
with very, very deep functionality.

00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:54.747
So I'll just give you some examples of the kind
of functionality that's available in SmallDocs.

00:01:55.407 --> 00:01:59.287
So, first of all, the documents, they are
originally rendered collapsed,

00:01:59.287 --> 00:02:03.267
so you can get an idea of what do I need to
look at now, and you can expand them all,

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:07.967
all the areas there, or expand the areas one by
one,

00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:10.407
or hit the H1 and expand everything.

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:16.867
They allow the agent to render very nice
diagrams, for example a Mermaid diagram,

00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:20.007
and you can get everything in its own nice
interface.

00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:23.487
And one of the things that is also really
important about SmallDocs

00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:25.067
is agent-to-human feedback loops.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:28.527
So all the little features come with things
that make your life easier to either

00:02:28.527 --> 00:02:32.427
communicate ideas to humans or communicate
ideas to other agents or to the agent that
you're working with.

00:02:32.427 --> 00:02:35.847
So, for example, on a diagram, you can copy the
source and then discuss it with an agent.

00:02:35.847 --> 00:02:40.867
For example, I could paste that in there, or I
can copy the diagram and I can send that into a
chat.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:45.827
It can be a small little thing. So, for
example, when an agent's made a table and I
want to copy a column

00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:49.267
or I want to copy the whole table as a CSV or
copy the whole table as a PNG, I can do that
too.

00:02:51.187 --> 00:02:55.207
So they're filled with little useful things to
make your life easier, basically.

00:02:56.427 --> 00:02:57.667
Here's another example of a SmallDoc.

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.067
You can see another Mermaid diagram.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:02.427
They render charts. They render math. They
render multi-tab spreadsheets.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.767
So an agent can actually do a bit of work for
you

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:08.647
and you can treat that as an actual spreadsheet
and interact with it

00:03:08.787 --> 00:03:11.687
and do what you need with it.

00:03:11.707 --> 00:03:16.987
The idea is that we keep tackling super kind
of, we tackle

00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:20.247
so many different tasks with our agents. And
the document format that we work with, I think,

00:03:20.247 --> 00:03:22.287
should be as flexible as this vast range of
tasks

00:03:22.287 --> 00:03:26.287
and not siloed into this is a spreadsheet, this
is some slides, this is some code.

00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:27.627
I think everything lives together.

00:03:27.627 --> 00:03:29.027
And in SmallDocs, that's how it works.

00:03:29.247 --> 00:03:32.647
And I found that that really allows me to get
the agent to communicate to me

00:03:32.647 --> 00:03:36.427
in an effective way that I can understand
better. And of course, we render code.

00:03:37.947 --> 00:03:43.247
The agent can make extremely visually appealing
slides and it has really good defaults built
in.

00:03:43.247 --> 00:03:48.327
So if you just ask for a deck, it can generate
nice looking presentations, which are,

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:52.187
I think, very human readable. And you can even
use presentations not to present to someone.

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:53.807
I use presentations a lot to understand things.

00:03:53.807 --> 00:03:57.427
So I'll say to the agent, okay, make one slide
for me for this idea,

00:03:57.927 --> 00:04:00.607
all the different parts of this idea, and I can
ingest it one by one.

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:05.287
So it's kind of a creative tool to allow your
agent to communicate to you in a way that

00:04:05.287 --> 00:04:07.287
maximizes your rate of understanding because we
all have

00:04:07.287 --> 00:04:10.967
so much to understand these days with different
work tasks that are very complicated

00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:16.887
with the agent who is extremely clever doing
work beyond our kind of where we're at at that
moment.

00:04:16.907 --> 00:04:20.587
So they're deep, they're flexible, they're
fast, and they're lightweight.

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:22.987
SmallDocs also comes with a library.

00:04:23.927 --> 00:04:27.407
And every library has a personal, every user
has a personal library, which you can't add
anyone to.

00:04:27.407 --> 00:04:28.787
So this is just for your personal documents.

00:04:28.927 --> 00:04:31.947
And there's also, you can create as many as you
want, team libraries.

00:04:32.707 --> 00:04:38.427
And you can search for documents in the
library, for example, penguins.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:42.267
And you can do things like filter for starred
documents, this kind of stuff.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:43.807
So it's a nice place where as you're working
with an agent,

00:04:43.807 --> 00:04:46.767
you're creating all these documents, you're
creating these kind of isolated blocks of
thinking

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.927
which you might share with other people or just
just for yourself.

00:04:48.987 --> 00:04:52.007
And you know that everything you've created is
saved and you can go back to it.

00:04:53.547 --> 00:04:57.747
You can, from this page, choose the library
that you want the document to be associated
with.

00:04:57.907 --> 00:05:00.127
You can choose who you want to share it with.

00:05:00.527 --> 00:05:03.367
And you can update the tags of the document to
match what you want.

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:07.647
But the agent is instructed to build a tagged
knowledge base for you.

00:05:07.647 --> 00:05:11.687
So you won't have to do anything. But just by
doing all your different bits of thinking,
creating documents,

00:05:12.507 --> 00:05:16.727
you will start to build a knowledge base that
you can query and also the agent can query.

00:05:16.727 --> 00:05:18.407
So the agent with your permissions,

00:05:18.427 --> 00:05:21.387
as in it can't read things that other people
have created that haven't shared with you,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:27.487
can use the tool to go to your library and find
what's available to you so you can get things
going.

00:05:28.147 --> 00:05:29.867
And so that's where sharing documents within
your library.

00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:31.807
And you can also share them outside of your
library,

00:05:31.807 --> 00:05:33.447
either a page that can be indexed by Google

00:05:33.447 --> 00:05:36.267
or a private link that you can just share with
someone that Google can't pick up.

00:05:38.027 --> 00:05:42.467
It's also possible to work with an agent

00:05:43.007 --> 00:05:47.487
to get feedback going on. So let's use this
document.

00:05:48.287 --> 00:05:54.387
And so I could say something like to the agent.

00:05:56.787 --> 00:05:58.507
This area isn't clear.

00:05:59.887 --> 00:06:01.747
Instead, use two slides.

00:06:01.747 --> 00:06:03.607
And you can use voice to speak if you want to.

00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:07.707
So now I've left a comment for the agent.

00:06:07.707 --> 00:06:09.727
And I can either if I want just copy with
comments and the agent

00:06:09.727 --> 00:06:13.567
and paste it into the agent chat box, or if you
want, the agent can also pick up on comments.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:17.507
So I could say clear this text box.

00:06:26.247 --> 00:06:29.547
And the agent can interact with this cloud
document.

00:06:29.687 --> 00:06:31.207
And it can find the comment.

00:06:31.447 --> 00:06:35.767
And then it can update it. And as the agent
updates it, you'll get a...

00:06:35.927 --> 00:06:37.607
it'll change the document we're working on
together.

00:06:38.327 --> 00:06:40.027
And you'll get a change log.

00:06:40.027 --> 00:06:43.667
And the agent will also leave a reason as to
why it's changed things.

00:06:44.767 --> 00:06:47.807
While we're waiting for that, it's also just
worth pointing out little small things like

00:06:47.807 --> 00:06:51.687
the ability to copy things and that with
comments, you can leave them in a very detailed
way.

00:06:51.687 --> 00:06:55.587
So I could leave comment on a specific part of
this diagram, for example,

00:06:55.587 --> 00:06:57.327
if I wanted more information on it.

00:06:59.587 --> 00:07:02.067
So let's see, I'm sure the agent is working
away.

00:07:03.087 --> 00:07:05.687
And now it's editing the document with our
updated request.

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:12.307
The document's updated. And now we can see it's
made those slides there.

00:07:13.027 --> 00:07:15.717
And we have a change log where the agent has

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:19.487
updated things with two inline slides.

00:07:19.487 --> 00:07:21.087
So you can check how things have changed.

00:07:23.967 --> 00:07:27.287
One other little thing is here you can see
shared tags from your library and recent.

00:07:29.327 --> 00:07:33.887
The project is also open source. So the code is
on GitHub. Thanks very much.
